    <DataNode elementId="LINOLEIC_ACID" textLabel="linoleate" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="OYHQOLUKZRVURQ-HZJYTTRNSA-M" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="414.73345947265625" centerY="1172.1171875" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="xue, PMN, nilo-poyanco">|FRAME: LINOLEIC_ACID Linoleate| (linoleic acid) is an &amp;omega;-6 polyunsaturated fatty acid with an 18-carbon chain and two &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt; double bonds at positions 9 and 12. Linoleate is produced by many organisms, including bacteria, plants, fungi and some insects. It is abundant in many vegetable oils, especially safflower and sunflower oils. While mammals require linoleate, which is found in their membranes and is used as the precursor for production of |FRAME: CPD-8117|, |FRAME: ARACHIDONIC_ACID|, |FRAME: Prostaglandins prostaglandins|, |FRAME: Leukotrienes leukotrienes|, and |FRAME: Thromboxanes thromboxanes|, they are not able to produce it since they lack |FRAME: EC-1.14.19.6|. Thus linoleate is an essential fatty acid, and must be obtained through the diet. Some of the compounds directly derived from linoleate are listed |FRAME: Linoleate-Derived-Metabolites here|. Like other fatty acids, |FRAME: LINOLEIC_ACID| is rarely found in its free form. It is usually found either as |FRAME: CPD-18| or |FRAME: Linoleoyl-groups &quot;incorporated into a lipid&quot;|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="LINOLEIC_ACID" />
